  "worked_solution": "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/5b2ab039994e7c2306cad7e30717fb136b5dfc3a028db284a0e95f49b56d580f.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,56)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(18,48).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{48-56}{18-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{16} \\\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,56):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+b \\\\ 56 & =-0.5(2)+b \\\\ 56 & =-1+b \\\\ 57 & =b \\\\ b & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+57.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=40$ into the equation above and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+57 \\\\ 40 & =-0.5x+57 \\\\ 0.5x+40 & =57 \\\\ 0.5x & =17 \\\\ x & =34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the population will be $40\\,000$ around the year $2000+34=2034.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=40$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable.",
